<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saarbrücken</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saarbrücken is the capital of the German state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saarland">Saarland</a>, on the Saar river near the French border. Seat of medieval counts, it passed in the 14th century to a line of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-nassau">House of Nassau</a>, whose 18th-century princes made it a baroque residence with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwigskirche">Ludwigskirche</a>. A centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> and iron in the industrial age, it went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1815 and shared the Saar's 20th-century changes between German and French control.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saarbrucken.jpg" alt="Saarbrücken" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Calips · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saarland_Rathaus_Saarbr%C3%BCcken_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The County of <b>Saarbrücken</b> passed by marriage to the Walramian Nassaus when Count John I of the County of Nassau-Weilburg married its heiress Johanna.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-nassau-saarbrucken">County of Nassau-Saarbrücken</a></li><li><blockquote>John acquired <b>Saarbrücken</b> by marriage, and the family built up scattered territories across the middle Rhine and Saar.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-nassau-weilburg">County of Nassau-Weilburg</a></li><li><blockquote>The original line died out in 1220, and the lands passed through a sister to the <b>Saarbrücken</b> family, who took the Leiningen name.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-leiningen">Principality of Leiningen</a></li></ul></section>
